3c — Write-up
Yes, the ranks changed substantially between the raw-count and per-1,000 charts. Brooklyn led in raw complaints (88,591) but fell to 3rd per 1,000 (32.38), while the Bronx 
rose from 2nd in raw counts to 1st per 1,000 (50.71) and Manhattan rose from 4th to 2nd (36.57); Queens dropped from 3rd to 4th, and Staten Island remained last. 
This reflects borough size: larger populations (Brooklyn, Queens) can produce high raw counts even when the rate per resident is lower. The Bronx’s higher rate may relate to differences in housing stock, 
building/heating conditions, and reporting patterns, and Manhattan’s rate may reflect its dense mix of residential and commercial activity—hypotheses rather than facts the data can prove, with the reminder 
that rates still measure 311 calls, not people or underlying conditions.
